  "_design": "Four to six thousand people living above a marsh, and the map is built so that water is the default and every walkable tile is something somebody had to pay for. It is painted in that order too: the whole rectangle is open water, then the boat channel, then the piling field, then the platforms, then the nine walks that join them. Nothing here is a street grid with posts drawn under it. The gaps ARE the town, and the shape of the place is which platforms happen to be joined to which.",
  "_plan": "Two levels, said in materials rather than in height, because a tile map has no height. STONE is the old town: the Rock is the only stone within a day of here, it is why there is a settlement at all, and it carries the Sanctum, the Kin Hall and both chartered buildings. PLANK is everything after -- the Landing, the West Quay, the three South Rows, the East Stage -- lower, wetter, and where the working end of the town lives. Crossing between them is going up or down without one tile of it being drawn in perspective, and the two ramps either side of the Rock are the stairs.\n\nThe gaps do the rest of the work. The West Quay hangs off ONE bridge, two tiles long, at row 29. The three South Rows read as one street from the Rock and are three separate decks joined end to end, so walking from the first to the third is four times the distance it looks. The East Stage is four tiles from the Landing across the boat channel and thirty-eight on foot, which is why everybody here owns a punt. And the oldest lamp in Mirehaven is still burning on a stage in the north-east that cannot be walked to at all: visible from the causeway, four rows of deep water out, and shut until the player can swim.",
